Icons are drawn as they were authored

The home screen shows four entries, and until now no two of them could look
alike, because two different pieces of machinery drew them:

  * an application's icon goes through LauncherIcons.createBadgedIconBitmap().
    With LEGACY_ICON_TREATMENT, an icon that is not an AdaptiveIconDrawable and
    whose application targets O or later is *wrapped* in one -- a white rounded
    tile with the icon scaled into the adaptive safe zone, two thirds of the
    width -- and then ADAPTIVE_ICON_SHADOW bakes a shadow under it;
  * the all-applications entry is a legacy shortcut, so LoaderCursor.loadIcon()
    calls LauncherIcons.createIconBitmap(), which does none of that and draws
    the drawable as it is.

So the three applications were white tiles with a shadow and the fourth was a
bare mark, and no amount of redrawing the fourth could make the four a set
("整个 ICON 没有完全平铺太大了，和其他 ICON 不匹配", the reader, 2026-10-22).
This turns both off, which is the half of the fix that belongs to the launcher:
every icon is now drawn as its author drew it, at the size the normalizer still
gives them all in common. The other half is the four marks themselves
(device/amazon/pw3/launcher-icons/, four of Lucide's).

The shadow is worth a line of its own. It is a blur rendered under each icon
and kept in the icon cache; on this screen it is dithered grey, which is what
docs/design/eink-ui.md rule 3 exists to keep off the panel, and it costs a blur
per icon at first load on a machine with one core.

Icon normalization is deliberately left on, and it is the one difference
between the two paths that remains: an application's icon is scaled by
IconNormalizer.getScale(), a legacy shortcut is not. It stays because it is
what keeps an application the reader installs from towering over these four,
and because on these four it is worth almost nothing. Running that method's
own arithmetic over the four marks as they are drawn at their intrinsic size
(48dp at density 1.875, so 90 px) gives 0.962 for the all-applications mark,
0.980 for Files, 1.000 for Settings and 0.994 for the Browser; the first of
those is the shortcut, so it is not applied, and the four end up within 2% of
each other, about two pixels of 112. The white tile was the whole of the
mismatch.

diff --git a/src/com/android/launcher3/config/BaseFlags.java b/src/com/android/launcher3/config/BaseFlags.java
--- a/src/com/android/launcher3/config/BaseFlags.java
+++ b/src/com/android/launcher3/config/BaseFlags.java
@@ -60,10 +60,16 @@ abstract class BaseFlags {
     // When enabled the status bar may show dark icons based on the top of the wallpaper.
     public static final boolean LIGHT_STATUS_BAR = false;
     // When enabled, icons not supporting {@link AdaptiveIconDrawable} will be wrapped in {@link FixedScaleDrawable}.
-    public static final boolean LEGACY_ICON_TREATMENT = true;
+    // PW3: off. Wrapping puts a white tile under an icon and shrinks it into the
+    // adaptive safe zone, which no legacy shortcut ever gets, so the home
+    // screen's four entries could not be drawn as one set.
+    public static final boolean LEGACY_ICON_TREATMENT = false;
     // When enabled, adaptive icons would have shadows baked when being stored to icon cache.
-    public static final boolean ADAPTIVE_ICON_SHADOW = true;
+    // PW3: off. The shadow is a blur baked under every icon; on this panel it is
+    // dithered grey (docs/design/eink-ui.md rule 3) and it costs a blur per icon
+    // on a single core.
+    public static final boolean ADAPTIVE_ICON_SHADOW = false;
     // When enabled, app discovery will be enabled if service is implemented
     public static final boolean DISCOVERY_ENABLED = false;
     // When enabled, the qsb will be moved to the hotseat.
     public static final boolean QSB_IN_HOTSEAT = true;
